(flip); markets price a Democratic win near 76%", "Epstein-files controversy and a complaint over Hegseth's campaign appearance dominate September coverage against Nunn", "No recent independent polls; the newest (March, R-sponsored) had Nunn narrowly ahead"], "rationale": "A favorable national environment, a slight fundraising and cash edge for Trone Garriott, and late-September coverage centered on the Epstein files dispute point to a narrow Democratic edge, though four raters still call it a tossup and the only recent poll showed Nunn narrowly ahead. Prediction markets at roughly 76 percent Democratic are consistent with a modest lead.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling or a fade in the Epstein-files story could shift the race back toward tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Zach Nunn (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Iowa's 3rd District, anchored by Des Moines and its western suburbs, leans Republican by about two points. First-term incumbent Zach Nunn faces state senator Sarah Trone Garriott, who holds a slight edge in both money raised and cash on hand. National forecasters are split: four call the race a tossup, five lean Democratic for a flip, and one rates it Likely Democratic. Prediction markets price a Democratic win around 76 to 77 percent.\n\nNo recent independent general-election polls exist; the newest, a Republican-sponsored March survey, had Nunn narrowly ahead. September coverage has centered on the Epstein files dispute, a complaint over Defense Secretary Hegseth appearing at a Nunn event, and Nunn's break with his party on Iran war powers, while Garriott has campaigned on the issue. A strong Democratic environment frames a close race with a modest Democratic edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7301, "margin": 3.78}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.755, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7544, "margin": 4.0}